The user wants a translation of C++ code to PHP.
The C++ code reads an integer n and a string s.
It iterates through the string.
If the character is uppercase, it calculates the size of a set `c` (which stores unique lowercase characters encountered since the last uppercase), updates `maxi`, and clears the set.
If the character is lowercase, it inserts it into the set `c`.
After the loop, it does a final check for the last segment.
It prints `maxi`.

PHP doesn't have a built-in `set` class like C++ `std::set` that automatically handles uniqueness and size efficiently in the same way, but `array_unique` or using keys of an associative array works.
Since we only need the count of unique lowercase letters in a segment, we can use an array as a hash map (keys are chars, values are true) or just collect chars and use `count(array_unique($arr))`.
However, doing `array_unique` on every uppercase char might be O(N^2) if implemented naively inside loop.
Better: maintain an associative array `$seen = [];` for the current segment.
When lowercase: `$seen[$char] = true;`
When uppercase: `$count = count($seen); $maxi = max($maxi, $count); $seen = [];`
At end: same logic.

Input reading: `fscanf(STDIN, "%d %s", $n, $s);` or `explode` on `fgets`.
The C++ code reads `n` then `s`. `n` is length of string, but in PHP we can just use `strlen($s)`.

Constraints: GNU C++14. PHP 7/8 compatible.

Let's write the PHP code.

```php
<?php
// Read input
$input = fgets(STDIN);
$parts = explode(' ', trim($input));
$n = (int)$parts[0];
$s = $parts[1] ?? ''; // Handle case where string might be on next line? C++ cin>>n>>s reads whitespace separated.
/* 
Actually, C++ cin >> n >> s reads integer then string (whitespace delimited).
The string might be on the next line.
So better to read all tokens.
*/
$tokens = [];
while (count($tokens) < 2) {
    $line = fgets(STDIN);
    if ($line === false) break;
    $tokens = array_merge($tokens, explode(' ', trim($line)));
}
$n = (int)$tokens[0];
$s = $tokens[1];

$maxi = 0;
$seen = []; // Acts as a set

for ($i = 0; $i < $n; $i++) {
    $char = $s[$i];
    if (ctype_upper($char)) {
        $size = count($seen);
        if ($size > $maxi) $maxi = $size;
        $seen = [];
    } else {
        $seen[$char] = true;
    }
}

$size = count($seen);
if ($size > $maxi) $maxi = $size;

echo $maxi;
?>
```

Wait, `ctype_upper` checks if *all* characters in string are uppercase. Since `$char` is a single character string, it works.
`ctype_lower` exists too. The C++ code uses `isupper`. `isupper` in C++ locale dependent but usually ASCII. `ctype_upper` in PHP is ASCII only basically.

The C++ code has `freq[26]` array unused (commented out). Ignore.

The logic seems straightforward.

One edge case: `n` might not be needed if we iterate string length, but C++ uses `n` as loop bound. PHP `$s` length should be `n`.

Let's verify input reading.
Standard competitive programming PHP input:
`fscanf(STDIN, "%d", $n);`
`fscanf(STDIN, "%s", $s);`
This handles newlines automatically.

So:
```php
<?php
fscanf(STDIN, "%d", $n);
fscanf(STDIN, "%s", $s);

$maxi = 0;
$seen = [];

for ($i = 0; $i < $n; $i++) {
    $ch = $s[$i];
    if (ctype_upper($ch)) {
        $maxi = max($maxi, count($seen));
        $seen = [];
    } else {
        $seen[$ch] = true;
    }
}

$maxi = max($maxi, count($seen));
echo $maxi;
?>
```
This is clean and matches logic exactly. `fscanf` reads whitespace separated tokens. `%s` reads until whitespace. Perfect.

Final check: `ctype_upper` returns true for 'A'-'Z'. `isupper` in C++ same.
`count($seen)` counts unique keys.
`$seen = []` clears.
`max` function works with integers.
Output `echo $maxi;`.

No extra description, just code block.

